      "computation_change_reason": "Default receiving-water versus mandatory synthetic dilution water depends on stream classification and zero-flow conditions; interpreting 'intermittent streams,' 'zero flow conditions,' or 'unaffected by the discharge' changes which monitoring protocol was required (B) and whether a period reflects conditional non-requirement or unresolved evidence (C)."
    },
    {
      "source": "aztec/final_permit.pdf",
      "locator": "Page: 10, Section: part_i",
      "exact_span": "If the receiving water is unsatisfactory as a result of instream toxicity (fails to fulfill the test acceptance criteria), the permittee may substitute synthetic dilution water for the receiving water in all subsequent tests provided the unacceptable receiving water test met the following stipulations:\n                i. a synthetic dilution water control which fulfills the test acceptance require-\n                   ments was run concurrently with the receiving water control;\n               ii. the test indicating receiving water toxicity has been carried out to\n                   completion,\n              iii. the permittee includes all test results indicating receiving water toxicity\n                   with the full report and information required; and\n              iv. the synthetic dilution water shall have a pH, hardness, and alkalinity\n                   similar to that of the receiving water or closest downstream perennial\n                   water not adversely affected by the discharge, provided the magnitude of\n                   these parameters will not cause toxicity in the synthetic dilution water.",
      "affected_purpose": "A,B,C",
      "computation_change_reason": "Substitution after instream-toxicity failure is permissive ('may substitute') and conditioned on concurrent controls, completed tests, reporting, and water-quality similarity; different readings of whether those stipulations were met change the valid test protocol (B), whether resulting toxicity results are enforceable for limit comparison (A), and whether missing reports or incomplete tests are documented exceptions versus inadequate evidence (C)."
    }
  ]
}
